Q: Is there a good online compiler for C quadruple precision float calculations, and what sample code can be used to test it? A: <h2>Using on-line compiler with __float128</h2>
<p>My search result is exactly like yours which also lands on Godbolt &amp; OnlineGDB and just like you discovered, it require a linker flag <code>-lquadmath</code>.</p>
<p>For <a href="https://godbolt.org/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">Godbolt</a>, do these steps:</p>
<ol>
<li>On the left panel, type any valid code using <code>__float128</code>.
<ul>
<li>In the language menu, select <code>C</code></li>
</ul>
</li>
<li>On the right panel, select <code>x86-64 gcc 16.2</code> from the compiler list.
<ul>
<li>In &quot;Compiler options...&quot; box, enter <code>-lquadmath</code></li>
<li>In &quot;Output...&quot; menu, select checkbox &quot;Execute the code&quot;</li>
</ul>
</li>
<li>At the bottom, click the &quot;Output&quot; button to show the output window.</li>
<li>If the result is not there, click the spin-circle sign (&quot;Clear cache &amp; recompile&quot;) so it can rebuild again, the result should be displayed in output window, if the code is correct.</li>
</ol>
<p>So, Godbolt can use quadruple precision float type <code>__float128</code> successfully. If you encountered a problem, one of the reasons could be that you chose a compiler other than <strong>GCC</strong>.</p>
<h2>Test code</h2>
<pre class="lang-c prettyprint-override"><code>#include &lt;quadmath.h&gt;
#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;

int main() {
    __float128 x = 2.0Q;
    __float128 y = M_PIq / x;
    char buf[128];
    quadmath_snprintf(buf, sizeof buf, &quot;%.40Qg&quot;, y);
    printf(&quot;π/2 = %s\n&quot;, buf);
    printf(&quot;validation = %d\n&quot;, y == M_PI_2q );
    return 0;
}
</code></pre>
<p>Expected output:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>π/2 = 1.57079632679489661923132169163975139874
validation = 1
</code></pre>
<h2>Off-line alternative</h2>
<p>Compiling C code using GCC using quadruple precision float <code>__float128</code> should not be a problem, just remember to include the option <code>-lquadmath</code> when building the binary executable. But GCC might not be always available on platforms other than Linux.
The best alternative you can find is using the <a href="https://www.mpfr.org/" rel="nofollow noreferrer">GNU MPFR Library</a>. It's portable, supported by most major C compilers, and can have multiple-precision floating-point, not just quadruple.</p>
<p>As for the tests that you mentioned, MPFR its own source code is hosted <a href="https://gitlab.inria.fr/mpfr/mpfr" rel="nofollow noreferrer">here</a> on gitlab and it comes with its own <a href="https://gitlab.inria.fr/mpfr/mpfr/-/tree/master/tests?ref_type=heads" rel="nofollow noreferrer">tests</a>.</p>
